OpenAmika: Fully Local Setup Guide (24 GB NVIDIA GPUs)
By default, running OpenAmika 100% locally (Brain + Voice + Ears + Memory + Camera Eyes) easily exceeds 26–29 GB of VRAM, causing a 24 GB card (like an RTX 3090 or RTX 4090) to spill memory into slow system RAM, spike to 94%+ GPU utilization, and freeze mid-conversation.
This guide shows you how to run OpenAmika 100% locally, smoothly, and in-character with her voice and camera vision enabled on a 24 GB GPU
Why This Setup Works (The VRAM Budget)
Instead of letting every single service fight for your GPU memory at the same time, this setup puts the light background tasks on your CPU and keeps only the heavy real-time AI tasks on your NVIDIA GPU:
•	Amika's Ears (speech_device) & Memory (embedder): Moved to your CPU 
(0 GB VRAM). Modern CPUs handle voice recognition and text embeddings effortlessly without causing sudden 2 GB VRAM spikes on your graphics card.

•	Amika's Voice (tts — GPT-SoVITS): Kept on your GPU (~2.2 GB VRAM) for fast, real-time voice generation.

•	Amika's Camera Eyes (smolvlm2-500m): Kept on your GPU (~1.2 GB VRAM), tuned to take 1 snapshot every ~3 seconds so it never interrupts her while she is speaking.

•	Amika's Brain (gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat): Kept on your GPU (~16 GB VRAM). Because Gemma 4 26B is a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) model with 26 billion parameters total but only ~4 billion active per word, she keeps her full personality and bilingual prompt formatting while replying at the speed of a tiny 4B model.
Total GPU VRAM Used: ~20.5 to 23.5 GB / 24.0 GB (leaving a safe buffer for Windows and your browser).
STEP 1 — Stop NVIDIA "Shared GPU Memory" Lockups
When a game or AI model gets close to 24 GB of VRAM, NVIDIA's default driver silently spills the extra gigabytes into Shared GPU Memory (your PC's normal system RAM over the PCIe bus). For a local language model, even 1 GB of Shared GPU Memory spillover slows token generation by 90% and pins your GPU at 92%–99% utilization until OpenAmika times out.
1.	Right-click an empty spot on your Windows desktop and open NVIDIA Control Panel.
2.	In the left sidebar, click Manage 3D Settings.
3.	Under the Global Settings tab, scroll down to CUDA - Sysmem Fallback Policy.
4.	Change it from Driver Default to Prefer No Sysmem Fallback.
5.	Click Apply in the bottom-right corner.
Why this is required: Setting Prefer No Sysmem Fallback forbids the NVIDIA driver from dumping model weights into slow Shared GPU Memory. On a 24 GB card, it keeps everything strictly inside fast GDDR6X VRAM; on a 16 GB card, it forces Ollama to cleanly split leftover layers onto your CPU instead of stalling the graphics driver.


STEP 2 — Set Windows Environment Variables for Ollama
By default, Ollama reserves memory for 4 parallel conversations at once in uncompressed 16-bit precision, wasting ~4.5 GB of VRAM on empty memory slots. Adding these five User Environment Variables cuts Ollama's memory overhead in half.
1.	Press the Windows key, type environment variables, and click Edit environment variables for your account (or open System Properties -> Advanced -> Environment Variables...).
2.	Under the top section (User variables for <Your Name>), click New... and add each of the following five variables:
Variable Name	Value	Why You Need It
OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION	1	Enables Flash Attention, which speeds up long prompt processing and lowers VRAM spikes while Amika reads her character sheet.

OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE	-1	Keeps the language model permanently loaded in VRAM (-1 = forever). Without this, Ollama unloads her brain after 5 minutes of silence, causing a 20-second freeze the next time you speak.

OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE	q4_0	Compresses her 16k conversation memory cache from 16-bit (f16) to 4-bit (q4_0). This saves ~2.5 GB of VRAM with near-zero impact on her memory accuracy.

OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS	1	Forces Ollama to hold only 1 language model in VRAM at a time. If you test a different model, Ollama immediately unloads the old one first.

OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL	1	Stops Ollama from reserving 4 parallel 16k context buffers in VRAM. Setting this to 1 saves ~3 GB of VRAM immediately.

3.	Click OK to close the windows.
4.	Restart Ollama so it reads the new variables: right-click the Ollama llama icon in your bottom-right Windows taskbar tray, click Quit Ollama, and launch Ollama again from the Start menu.
 
STEP 3 — Download the 16 GB QAT Build of Gemma 4 26B
Models smaller than 27B normally break Amika's reply format or repeat lines from her system prompt. Gemma 4 26B MoE is the one exception tested by her creator that holds her full character.
However, the default gemma4:26b tag pulls a heavier ~19 GB file. Instead, pull Google's official QAT (Quantization-Aware Trained) build, which shrinks the download to 16 GB while preserving full 4-bit precision:
1.	Open Command Prompt (cmd) or PowerShell and run:
2.	ollama pull gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat

3.	(Optional) If you previously downloaded larger models you no longer use, remove them so Ollama never loads them by mistake:
4.	ollama list
5.	ollama rm <old_model_name>

