Why Each settings.json Change Matters
•	"speech_device": "cpu" — Moves her microphone speech-to-text engine (SenseVoice) off your graphics card onto your CPU. When left on "cuda:0", speaking while she is thinking creates sudden 1.5–2 GB VRAM spikes that can crash the language model. On "cpu", she hears you immediately with zero VRAM cost.

•	"embedder": { "device": "cpu" } — Moves her long-term memory search model (BAAI/bge-m3) to your CPU, saving another ~1.5 GB of VRAM.

•	"vision": { "model": "smolvlm2-500m" } — Uses only ~1.2 GB of VRAM instead of the 4.3 GB required by qwen3-vl-2b, saving over 3 GB of VRAM so the 26B brain never hits the 24 GB ceiling.

•	"max_tokens": 45 — Gives the camera model enough words to finish a complete sentence about what you are doing without getting cut off mid-sentence or rambling.

•	"max_dps": 0.3 — Tells the camera to describe a new webcam frame once every ~3.3 seconds (0.3 descriptions per second) instead of every 1.0 second. Polling the camera every single second constantly interrupts the GPU while she is trying to generate her reply.
STEP 5 — Starting Her Up & Selecting the Model
1.	Double-click Start_OpenAmika.bat and wait until all rows in her console window turn green (ready).
2.	In your browser (http://localhost:53000 or http://localhost:53010), open Setup -> Brain.
3.	Click Ollama, click Detect models, select gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat, and click Use this model.
4.	(If you previously tested smaller 14B models that got stuck repeating lines): Still on the Setup page, scroll down to Danger zone and click Wipe memory so she starts with a clean conversation history.
5.	Go to the Use page, click the Camera button ON first (so the vision model locks its 1.2 GB of VRAM first), click the page once so the browser enables audio, press the Power button, and talk to her!
How to Verify Everything Is Running Properly
Open Command Prompt (cmd) while chatting with her and run:
ollama ps

•	On a 24 GB GPU (RTX 3090 / 4090), the PROCESSOR column will show 100% GPU, and Windows Task Manager (Performance -> GPU) will show Dedicated GPU memory sitting safely around 20.5–24.5 / 24.0 GB with Shared GPU memory near 0.0 GB.
 
 
